Description: khatru: policy check, then live listeners, then stored queries

Khatru handles every websocket message on its own goroutine and used to run
a REQ filter's stored query before registering its live listener. An event
saved while the query ran was therefore neither replayed (the query had
passed its point) nor broadcast (the listener did not exist yet): a live
subscriber missed it forever even though its publisher got OK true. A client
CLOSE arriving during a slow query also found nothing to remove, and the
listener lingered until disconnect.

Fix: restructure the REQ branch into three phases and split handleRequest
into checkRequest + queryStoredEvents:

1. run the OnRequest acceptance policy for every filter, so a rejected REQ
   never registers a listener, not even transiently (a policy that gates
   reads, e.g. NIP-42 auth-required, must not expose events while the
   rejection is still being written);
2. register every live listener, still skipping ids-only filters;
3. run every stored query, then write EOSE.

An event saved during a query is now broadcast to the registered listener
and may also be emitted by the query's replay: clients deduplicate
duplicates per NIP-01. Servers with their own gap-buffer mechanism keyed to
the old ordering (listener added after the query) need to be updated; see
Bahia's internal/relaysidecar/fanout.go adaptation (drain moved to the end
of the query iterator).

Applied to Bahia's vendored copy; regression test:
khatru/listener_before_query_bahia_test.go
(TestListenerIsRegisteredBeforeTheStoredQuery, deterministic via a
QueryStored hook that publishes inside the query window).

